Critical behaviors & algorithms
1) Submit issue flow with similarity check

Client calls POST /issues:checkAndMaybeCreate with title/description/categories/tags/urgency/dates.

Server normalizes text (lowercase, strip HTML), vectorizes (title + description) using simple TF-IDF or embeddings (configurable).

Retrieve top-k similar issues (k=10, threshold=0.75 example).

Response returns:

similarIssues: [{issue, similarityScore}]

If none above threshold, server creates issue immediately and returns created entity.

If some above threshold, do not create yet; return suggestions for user decision.

If user selects an existing issue and claims it’s different:

User supplies differenceSummary.

Server creates a new Issue and:

Creates IssueHierarchy(parent=selectedIssue, child=newIssue).

Creates SimilarityLink(issueA=selectedIssue, issueB=newIssue, status=CONFIRMED, differenceSummary=...).

2) Upvotes and roll-up metric

Local upvotes: GET /issues/{id} includes localUpvotesCount.

Tree unique supporters:

Compute unique userId set across the node and all descendants (via DAG-safe traversal).

If a user upvoted both a parent and a child, count only once for the parent’s uniqueSupportersInTree.

Maintain a materialized roll-up table or cache updated via triggers/queues for performance.

3) similarityCount

similarityCount = count(SimilarityLink where status=CONFIRMED and (issueAId=issue.id OR issueBId=issue.id))

4) resolvesCount

Meaning: “How many other issues this would resolve if addressed.”

